<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE bindings PUBLIC "-//NetBeans//DTD Editor KeyBindings settings 1.1//EN"
                          "http://www.netbeans.org/dtds/EditorKeyBindings-1_1.dtd">
<!--
    Toggle Block Comment on VS Code's chord: Shift+Opt+A on macOS,
    Shift+Alt+A on Windows (and on Linux, beside VS Code's own Linux chord
    in vscode-block-comment-keybindings-linux.xml).

    Measured in the assembled cluster, editor and global keymaps, every
    profile on every OS family: Alt+Shift+A is free everywhere except in
    the Eclipse profile (toggle-rectangular-selection, O-S-A) and the
    NetBeans 5.5 profile (select-element-previous, O-S-A) on Windows and
    Linux, where O is Alt. On macOS O is Ctrl, so both leave it free
    there. So this file rides NetBeans, Emacs and Idea on every OS, and
    Eclipse and NetBeans55 on macOS only (their layer registrations carry
    nbeditor-settings-targetOS=OS_MAC); a profile's own meaning of a chord
    wins. No two-stroke binding starts with the chord.

    An EDITOR binding on purpose: on macOS Opt+Shift+A also types a
    character, and only a press the editor's own keymap consumed can have
    that typed echo swallowed (editing/TypedEcho).
-->
<!--
    The modifiers are written Shift first: the platform's keymap reader
    logs a WARNING at every start for a key that BEGINS with A or C in
    the default profile, and the order of the modifier letters means
    nothing to the key itself.
-->
<bindings>
    <bind actionName="nmox-toggle-block-comment" key="SA-A"/>
</bindings>
